{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"answers\":[{\"question_id\":\"Q018_tal_effector_repeat_lengths\",\"answer\":\"They share a homologous TAL-effector repeat architecture; SCOPe family membership does not require equal sequence length or equal repeat number.\\n\\nIn SCOPe 2.08, the genetic SEQRES domain sequences are 51 residues for d2kq5a_ (PthA), 373 for d3v6pa_ (dHax3), and 752 for d4cj9a_ (BurrH). SCOPe explicitly annotates d2kq5a_ as “single repeat” and marks d2kq5a_ and d3v6pa_ with the repeat-related exception “re”: the common domain can contain different numbers of small repeating units. Thus, a short repeat-containing fragment and much longer repeat arrays can belong to the same family. Construct boundaries, partial repeats, and flanking residues also affect length, so dividing total length by a typical repeat length does not establish exact repeat counts.\\n\\nAll three belong to family a.298.1.1, TAL (transcription activator-like) effector, within superfamily a.298.1, TAL effector-like; fold a.298, Left-handed alpha-alpha superhelix; and class a, All alpha proteins. Family membership supports a close evolutionary relationship, while superfamily membership supports broader common ancestry. Fold describes shared structural organization, and class describes secondary-structure composition; neither alone establishes homology.\\n\\nThe family’s canonical representative repeat is 4cj9 chain A residues 259–292, a 34-residue unit within d4cj9a_. This is a representative repeat, not the boundary of the entire 752-residue domain. Those coordinates apply to 4cj9 and must not be copied onto d2kq5a_ or d3v6pa_. Repeat-aware sequence and structural alignment is needed for template mapping; the 51-residue fragment cannot by itself represent the full geometry of a long array. Likewise, shared family membership does not establish identical DNA-target specificity or biological activity: those require recognition-residue comparisons and functional evidence. Canonical representative status does not establish that d4cj9a_ is the best template for every purpose.\",\"evidence_used\":[\"mcp__scope__get_scop_domains\",\"mcp__scope__get_scop_sequence\",\"/api/v1/releases/current\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d2kq5a_\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d2kq5a_/parents\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d2kq5a_/annotations\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d3v6pa_\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d3v6pa_/parents\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d3v6pa_/annotations\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d4cj9a_\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d4cj9a_/parents\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d4cj9a_/annotations\",\"/api/v1/sequences/sids/d2kq5a_?release=2.08&source=seqres&style=genetic&format=json\",\"/api/v1/sequences/sids/d3v6pa_?release=2.08&source=seqres&style=genetic&format=json\",\"/api/v1/sequences/sids/d4cj9a_?release=2.08&source=seqres&style=genetic&format=json\",\"Pretrained knowledge of TAL-effector repeats and SCOPe hierarchy interpretation\"]}]}"}}
